invalidationtracker: count RWX faults per page, because a global counter cannot
name the offender.

`0006`'s counter answered "is this happening at all" and returned zero, which is
what it was for. This is the next question — *which pages*, and how fast — and
the shape it had could not answer it.

One counter for the whole process, printed every 4096th fault. A Requiem session
took **3,656 RWX faults with 2,892 of them on one address**, `0x14FA2AFF0`, from
two program counters. It printed one line, naming whichever page happened to be
number 4096 — which was not the offender — and the thrash stayed invisible until
an `x86:everything` trace was grepped by hand. That is a firehose and a device
run to learn something the counter already had in it.

Per page, and named on powers of two. A page that faults once says so and then
goes quiet; one that climbs names itself at 1, 2, 4 … 2048, so the pathological
case costs twelve lines rather than seven hundred. The interesting fact is which
pages climb and how fast, not the total, and the total was the only thing the old
shape could report.

**It has to be able to return a negative**, which is why it stays silent for a
title that never repeats a page rather than printing a periodic heartbeat. A
session with nothing from this tag did not thrash, and that rules the mechanism
out in one run instead of leaving it a standing suspicion — the same property the
`EINTR` line in Vessel's X server was given for the same reason.

A guarded map rather than an atomic, because it has to be a map. This path takes
`CodeInvalidationMutex` three lines below and then makes an `NtProtectVirtualMemory`
call; a short uncontended lock is not what makes an SMC fault expensive.

**WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO IS FIX THE THRASH**, and the reason is worth
recording so the next person does not repeat the reasoning. The obvious fix —
"don't invalidate a whole page when the write is to data that shares it with
code" — was investigated and is already done: `LookupCache::InvalidateRange`
(`FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/LookupCache.h:125`) keys on `CodePages`, which is
per page, and no-ops when the page holds no translated block. So the cost is not
a wasted invalidation. It is the fault round trip, a write lock per code buffer,
and — the expensive part — the *recompilation* of everything on the page
afterwards, which then re-arms the trap for the next write.

Fixing that needs the overlap test at instruction granularity rather than page
granularity, which needs block *extents* where `CodePages` currently stores entry
addresses. That is a change to FEX's data structures, its failure mode is a stale
translation executing silently, and it should not be attempted on a hunch. This
patch is what makes the case for it measurable, and at the rate measured so far —
around ten to twenty faults a second, three orders of magnitude below the
"burns a core and renders nothing" bar in this file's own comment — the case is
not yet made.

